      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The Statutory Framework of Government Pensions in India</h2>
        <p>
          In the jurisprudence governing Indian public service, pensions are not an act of state bounty or ex-gratia charity. As affirmed in landmark constitutional rulings by the Supreme Court of India—most notably in <em>D.S. Nakara vs. Union of India</em>—pensions represent deferred compensation earned by public servants through decades of continuous, faithful service.
        </p>
        <p>
          For over sixty-seven lakh Central Government retirees, Defence veterans under One Rank One Pension (OROP), railway superannuated personnel, postal workers, and family pensioners, the constitution of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission</strong> is an essential economic milestone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unlike working employees who benefit from annual career increments, promotions, and variable allowances, senior citizens rely solely on their baseline superannuation allowance and biannual adjustments in <strong>Dearness Relief (DR)</strong>. Over a ten-year cycle, sustained retail inflation disproportionately impacts fixed-income retirees managing geriatric medical expenses. The 8th Central Pay Commission establishes revised basic pensions, minimum income floors, and restored purchasing power.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The Core Pension Equations: Past Pensioners vs Fresh Retirees</h2>
        <p>
          To compute pension entitlements accurately under the 8th Pay Commission, accounting rules distinguish between individuals already retired and staff retiring under the new pay scales:
        </p>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>Formula 1: For Existing Pensioners (Retired Prior to Implementation):</h4>
          <p class="formula-box">
            <strong>Revised 8th CPC Basic Pension</strong> = 7th CPC Basic Pension Recorded on PPO &times; Approved Fitment Factor
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Example:</em> A pensioner drawing a basic pension of ₹30,000 under the 7th CPC who receives a <strong>2.28 Fitment Factor</strong> will see their monthly basic pension rise directly to <strong>₹68,400 per month</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>Formula 2: For Fresh Retirees (Retiring After Implementation):</h4>
          <p class="formula-box">
            <strong>Full Basic Pension</strong> = 50% &times; [ Last Drawn Basic Pay or Average Emoluments (Last 10 Months), whichever is higher ]
          </p>
          <p>
            Under Rule 44 of the <strong>Central Civil Services (Pension) Rules, 2021</strong>, full 50% pension entitlement requires a minimum qualifying service of 20 years (previously 33 years before the 6th CPC reforms). Any employee fulfilling 20 years of continuous service receives the full 50% basic pension without proration.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

